[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last big question before I put a design together.

  Your original framing was "tasks they [1mcare about[0m." With a single user, every task is nominally yours — so "care about"
  has to mean something narrower, and how we define it decides whether the task model needs an extra field and whether
  the UI needs extra controls.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what marks a task as one that should reach you?

  - [1mHaving a due date is the signal.[0m You gave it a date, so you want to be reminded; no date, no reminder. Costs nothing
    — no new field, no new UI, and the concept is already there. The risk is noise if you routinely date things you
    don't actually care about.
  - [1mAn explicit star/flag.[0m A separate opt-in boolean per task. Matches your wording most literally and cleanly separates
    "when is this due" from "nag me about it." Costs one field and one control, and it's cheap to add later if
    dating-everything turns out to be noisy.
  - [1mA per-task reminder time.[0m You set an independent "remind me at" distinct from the due date. Most control — "due
    Friday, remind me Wednesday" — but it's the most UI, and it's the one that's awkward to simplify away once people
    rely on it.

  I'd go with [1mthe due date as the signal.[0m It's the YAGNI answer: it introduces no concept the app doesn't already need,
  and it makes the rule trivially explainable. Crucially it's also the cheap-to-change one — adding a star or a custom
  reminder time later is additive to the model, whereas starting with three overlapping "when do I get told" mechanisms
  and removing one means breaking stored data.
